# What each operator answers when it is handed operands of the wrong
# type, and which operators that question can be put to at all.
#
# DERIVED FROM THE SPECIFICATION. tests/plrm-errors records the errors
# PLRM 8.2 lists for each operator; this records which of them can be
# provoked, and that takes something the error lists do not carry: what
# type each operand is supposed to be. The manual does carry it, in the
# syntax line that opens every entry --
#
#     int string put
#     bool proc if
#     num1 num2 add
#
# -- where the operand names are typed by convention. So the same line
# that tells an entry from a mention also says what an operator takes,
# and the question can be put to every operator whose operands all have
# a definite type.
#
# ---- how it is asked
#
# By handing the operator nulls, one per operand the syntax line names.
# A null is of a type every named operand kind excludes, so the answer
# should be typecheck; and the COUNT is the count the line gives, so
# what answers is the type check and not the arity check. That second
# half is the trap. An operand list read short raises stackunderflow
# instead, which is a perfectly plausible-looking answer to a
# wrong-typed call, and a gate that accepted it would be testing
# nothing.
#
# ---- which operators the question cannot be put to
#
# skip rows, with the reason. Three kinds of syntax line put an operator
# out of reach: one naming no operands, one naming a variable number,
# and one whose operand is named any, obj, key or value -- kinds the
# specification lets be an object of any type, so a null is not wrong
# for them and nothing follows from the answer.
#
# procset rows name the operators that answer undefined to a bare call
# because they are not on any dictionary stack: each is a member of a
# procedure set, reached by finding that set as a resource. The set each
# belongs to is verified against the sets the interpreter publishes.
#
# The wrapped row is the one syntax line that runs across more than one
# physical line, so the extraction reads a suffix of its operands and
# derives a count too short. It is found two ways that agree: the probe
# answers stackunderflow, and the line above it in the text is operand
# words naming no operator.
#
# ---- when a different error is the right answer
#
# An operator may answer some other error before it looks at the type: a
# mark it counts down to and does not find, a current font it asks for
# first. Those are not failures and are not forced to typecheck; the row
# records the error, and the error is one the specification lists for
# that operator, checked against its entry rather than accepted because
# it looked reasonable.
#
# ---- the lines
#
#   drive   <name>  <operands>  <answer>  [<why the answer is not typecheck>]
#   procset <name>  <the procedure set or sets it belongs to>
#   wrapped <name>  <why its operand count cannot be read>
#   skip    <name>  <why the question cannot be put to it>
#   covered <n>     how many operators answer typecheck
#
# Every operator the specification defines has exactly one line here, so
# one that stops being defined, or arrives, is a line that no longer
# matches rather than a silence.

